  SECTION 6: SCALING ANALYSIS
  --------------------------------------------------

  Theoretical complexity:
    Naive:  O(k * n^3)    per crystallization run
    Fast:   O(n^3) init + O(k * n * log n)  per run
    Batch:  O(R * n^2) -> O(R * n^2/w)  with w-bit words
    Parallel: O(T_seq / P) for P independent groups

  Observed scaling (naive):
    n=7->10: time ratio=12.3x, n ratio=1.4x, exponent~=7.0
    n=10->15: time ratio=10.0x, n ratio=1.5x, exponent~=5.7
    n=15->20: time ratio=4.3x, n ratio=1.3x, exponent~=5.0
  Observed scaling (fast):
    n=7->10: time ratio=6.1x, n ratio=1.4x, exponent~=5.1
    n=10->15: time ratio=3.9x, n ratio=1.5x, exponent~=3.4
    n=15->20: time ratio=3.0x, n ratio=1.3x, exponent~=3.8

  SECTION 7: API SUMMARY
  --------------------------------------------------

  BitTournament(n, bits=0)
    .has_arc(a,b)     O(1) arc query
    .flip_arc(a,b)    O(1) arc flip
    .scores()         O(n^2) out-degrees
    .random(n)        O(1) random tournament
    .from_matrix(M)   O(n^2) convert from adjacency matrix
    .to_matrix()      O(n^2) convert to adjacency matrix

  IncrementalCrystallizer(tournament, margins)
    .crystallize(max_iter=10000)
    Returns: (final_tournament, iterations, flips_list)
    Complexity: O(n^3) init + O(k * n * log n) crystallization

  bitwise_majority(tournaments)
    Merge R tournaments via majority vote. O(R * n^2 / w).

  parallel_crystallize(groups, num_workers)
    Crystallize independent groups in parallel.
    groups: list of (BitTournament, margins) tuples

